1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:22,539
This is an account of the experience of the Consolidated Edison Company of New York,

2
00:00:22,539 --> 00:00:28,140
with the design, construction, and operation of one of the first nuclear power stations

3
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:30,240
serving a large metropolitan area.

4
00:00:34,399 --> 00:00:41,240
The project was begun in 1955 when there were doubts concerning uranium availability and supply.

5
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:47,640
And because thorium, a plentiful material,

6
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:51,140
offered possibilities for obtaining a higher rate of conversion

7
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:56,579
of fertile to fissionable material, it was decided to use thorium-232

8
00:00:56,579 --> 00:01:02,719
as the reactor's fertile material combined with uranium-235 as the fissionable.

9
00:01:06,659 --> 00:01:12,799
The projected system demand on Indian Point Station was 275 megawatts.

10
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:18,340
In 1955, this capacity could not be achieved in one reactor.

11
00:01:19,939 --> 00:01:26,500
So the final design called for reactor production of approximately 165 megawatts.

12
00:01:26,579 --> 00:01:36,159
with the remainder to be supplied by conventional super heaters.

13
00:01:36,159 --> 00:01:42,659
These super heaters provided additional capacity at low capital cost, plus a significant reduction

14
00:01:42,659 --> 00:01:48,939
in the turbine cycle heat rate.

15
00:01:48,939 --> 00:01:56,379
To achieve 165 megawatts, the average power density was set at 76 kilowatts per liter.

16
00:01:56,379 --> 00:02:01,299
highest of any central station power reactor operating today with a projected

17
00:02:01,299 --> 00:02:12,979
core life of 470 effective full-power days the high power density and use of

18
00:02:12,979 --> 00:02:18,560
thorium as fertile material about which there was then scant actual operational

19
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:23,439
data made necessary a series of critical experiments to confirm design

20
00:02:23,439 --> 00:02:31,520
calculations. These experiments were conducted at the Babcock and Wilcox

21
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:35,319
Nuclear Facility in Virginia.

22
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:42,520
Two particular problem areas were uncovered. One, the ratio of peak to

23
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:49,900
average power in the core exceeded design value of 4.2 by about 25%. This

24
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:55,620
This was solved by incorporating variable U-235 concentrations within the fuel rods

25
00:02:55,620 --> 00:03:02,759
in each element and by zone loading.

26
00:03:02,759 --> 00:03:09,060
The uranium loading varies in each of three zones.

27
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:14,540
Lowest in the center zone of the core.

28
00:03:14,540 --> 00:03:19,759
Increased in the next outer zone.

29
00:03:19,759 --> 00:03:21,699
greatest in the outside zone.

30
00:03:22,399 --> 00:03:27,459
This combination of zone loading and variable uranium concentration within each fuel element

31
00:03:27,459 --> 00:03:33,120
resulted in a ratio of peak to average power that satisfied design requirements.

32
00:03:37,759 --> 00:03:44,819
The second problem area had to do with the reactivity control capability of the 21 movable control rods,

33
00:03:45,319 --> 00:03:47,979
inadequate for the desired long core lifetime.

34
00:03:49,759 --> 00:03:57,959
This problem was solved by supplementing the movable control rods with boric acid in the

35
00:03:57,959 --> 00:04:07,939
moderator to hold down reactivity due to moderator temperature change by adding approximately

36
00:04:07,939 --> 00:04:15,099
225 parts per million of natural boron to the fuel element cladding to serve as burnable

37
00:04:15,099 --> 00:04:25,319
poison and by installing 12 boron stainless steel fixed shim rods between the fuel elements.

38
00:04:25,319 --> 00:04:33,319
These fixed shim rods are removed at about half core lifetime.

39
00:04:33,319 --> 00:04:38,360
The Indian Point reactor is required to meet the same load changes as are imposed on conventional

40
00:04:38,360 --> 00:04:41,600
units.

41
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:46,700
The coupling of super heaters with the reactor dictates that the steam pressure remains nearly

42
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:49,060
constant at all times.

43
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:54,900
This presented design problems in reactor and plant control.

44
00:04:54,900 --> 00:05:02,360
A special automatic control system was developed to meet these problems.

45
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:07,800
This automatic system, unlike other nuclear power plant controls, utilizes signals from

46
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:13,079
steam pressure and flow to adjust reactor power output to match generator load.

47
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:19,160
The ability of the control system to follow rapid load changes

48
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:22,280
eliminates need for manual adjustment by the operator.

49
00:05:26,439 --> 00:05:31,160
Knowledge of reactor and combined reactor conventional superheated transients was limited.

50
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:42,460
Therefore, the system was designed for at least 9% per minute ramp changes and at least

51
00:05:42,460 --> 00:05:47,940
18% step changes.

52
00:05:47,940 --> 00:05:56,140
Actual control and response of the system indicates that ramp changes of 15% per minute

53
00:05:56,140 --> 00:06:00,100
and step changes of almost 30% are possible.

54
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:10,879
approximately 50% above design specifications and exceeding most conventional plant capabilities.

55
00:06:10,879 --> 00:06:16,800
To summarize, the Indian Point Reactor, the first power reactor in the world utilizing

56
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:26,540
thorium and enriched uranium, had through January 1964 generated 1,173,420 megawatt

57
00:06:26,540 --> 00:06:35,700
of electricity. And every indication is that the reactor has excess thermal

58
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:43,700
capacity. Thus the Indian Point experience has confirmed the use of

59
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:51,080
thorium as a fertile material. And equally important, is opening the door to

60
00:06:51,079 --> 00:06:58,419
refabrication of U-233 as a reactor fuel.

61
00:06:58,419 --> 00:07:03,939
Important too is that Indian Point after early problems principally on the

62
00:07:03,939 --> 00:07:09,360
conventional side of the plant is in commercial operation achieving load

63
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:18,579
factors comparable to Con Edison's conventional plants. Further, Indian

64
00:07:18,579 --> 00:07:24,199
Indian Point's operating costs, like its load factor, are already almost equal to the average

65
00:07:24,199 --> 00:07:29,699
of the system's conventional plants, and it is expected that future operating costs will

66
00:07:29,699 --> 00:07:34,319
be among the lowest in the Con Edison system.

67
00:07:34,319 --> 00:07:44,819
In short, Indian Point is a long step forward in the practical application of peaceful use

68
00:07:44,819 --> 00:07:54,699
of nuclear power. Less than 35 miles from the heart of New York City.
